# Postmortem

Our team went further than simply fixing these issues. We conducted a
*postmortem* to understand *how* we introduced these vulnerabilities in our
releases and *what* we could do to avoid similar vulnerabilities in the future.
This analysis lead to technical, policy, and culture changes.

This analysis was useful and we'll definitely consider doing postmortems again
after future audits. It might also be useful for other projects to understand
how we worked on these long-lasting improvements.

## Technical improvements

 - Our `sudo` configuration is now generated from a higher-level description,
   which has safer default settings and demands explanations when diverging
   from them ([[!tails_mr 1979]]).

   This will make it much harder to introduce vulnerabilities such as
   [[!tails_ticket 19595]], [[!tails_ticket 20709]], and [[!tails_ticket
   20710]].

 - Our CI now ensures that all included Tails-made Python executables run in
   isolated mode ([[!tails_mr 1911]]).

   This defends against issues like [[!tails_ticket 20702]] and [[!tails_ticket 20744]].

 - Our CI now ensures that we don't write software that does unsafe `.desktop`
   files lookup ([[!tails_mr 2040]]).

   This defends against issues like [[!tails_ticket 20733]] and [[!tails_ticket 20745]].

 - While we prepare Tails N+1.0 based on next version of Debian,
   we will look for Perl code, included in the Tails image,
   that suspiciously modifies `@INC`.

   This defends against issues like [[!tails_ticket 20701]].

 - We will periodically audit our `onion-grater` configuration.

## Policy & culture improvements

 - While the security audit process was ongoing, we noticed that we lacked
   a shared, documented understanding of whether, and when, confidential
   security issues shall be made public.

   This caused a variety of problems. First, team members were working with
   different implicit assumptions, which caused a couple of communication
   mishaps. Second, during this audit we have been erring on the side of
   secrecy. This felt better as a temporary measure, in order to be "on the safe
   side", to protect our users on the short term — but without a disclosure
   process, we were not meeting our own standards in terms of transparency and
   openness to third-party reviews of our work.

   So we created our [security issue response
   policy](https://tails.net/contribute/security_policy/), based on the Tor
   network team's one. It includes, in particular, guidelines for
   confidentiality & disclosure.

 - We decided to be more intentional, as a team, about when it's worth the
   effort & risk to do a big code refactoring.

   While we recognize that refactoring is a necessary part of a healthy software
   development process, this postmortem showed that large refactorings also
   provide opportunities for security vulnerabilities to sneak into our code
   base, under the radar.

 - For merge requests that include security-sensitive changes, such as
   creating/modifying `sudo` configuration or code that elevates privileges:
   we will now require an extra, *security-focused* review.

 - We will share knowledge and awareness about security issues more broadly
   within the team when we discover them, so that every team member has
   opportunities to learn along the way.
